<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Anthem · c. 1929 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Guantanamera</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Cuban song set to verses by the poet José Martí, sung around the world as a song of love for Cuba.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>José Martí (verses); Joseíto Fernández (credited)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Joseíto Fernández (sole composer by a 1993 ruling; Herminio García Wilson also claimed)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Cubans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cuba</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1929 (popularized on radio by Joseíto Fernández)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (the verses are José Martí’s Versos sencillos (1891); the refrain and the music, credited to Joseíto Fernández (he died in 1979), are in copyright and not printed)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Guantanamera&quot;, Spanish for &quot;the woman from Guantánamo&quot;, is a Cuban patriotic song whose verses come from poems by the Cuban poet José Martí. The official writing credit goes to Joseíto Fernández, who popularized it on the radio from as early as 1929 and used it regularly in one of his programmes; it is not clear when it was first released on record. Fernández claimed to have written the music at various dates, and the consensus places its origin in 1929. Some hold that the song's structure came from Herminio &quot;El Diablo&quot; García Wilson, who could be credited as co-composer; his heirs went to court decades later and lost, and in 1993 the People's Supreme Court of Cuba named Fernández sole composer of the music. On either account, Fernández was the first to spread the song widely.</p><p>The version known outside Cuba joined Martí's verses to the tune. Pete Seeger based his reworking on a version by Martí and Julián Orbón, as performed by Héctor Angulo, and meant it for the peace movement at the time of the Cuban Missile Crisis: he urged people to sing it, in Spanish, as a sign of unity between the American and Cuban peoples. The Weavers sang it at their Carnegie Hall reunion concert in May 1963, and shortly afterwards Seeger put it on his live album We Shall Overcome, a recording AllMusic's Stewart Mason calls the definitive version.</p><p>The Sandpipers' 1966 recording, based on the Weavers' arrangement, arranged by Mort Garson and produced by Tommy LiPuma, became an international hit, reaching No. 9 on the Billboard Hot 100 and No. 7 in Britain. Celia Cruz (1925–2003) made it one of her signature songs: it appears on at least 241 of her records and compilations, from a 1968 recording on Tico Records onward, and her posthumous autobiography of 2004 recalls singing it nine times. Wyclef Jean's version, with Celia Cruz, Jeni Fujita and a rap verse by Lauryn Hill, added new words and music and was nominated for a Grammy Award.</p><p>The song has been recorded by many others, from Joan Baez, Julio Iglesias and Nana Mouskouri to Inti-Illimani, the Buena Vista Social Club and the Gipsy Kings; Bárbara y Dick took it to number one in Argentina. Its tune is also widely used for British football chants, and the Pakistani singer Alamgir adapted it in the 1980s with Urdu words as &quot;Albela Rahi&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of love for Cuba and of solidarity with the poor of the world. Its mood is warm and patriotic.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Yo soy un hombre sincero</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am an honest man</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De donde crece la palma.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from where the palm tree grows,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y antes de morirme quiero</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and before I die I want</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Echar mis versos del alma.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to pour out the verses of my soul.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Mi verso es de un verde claro</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My verse is of a light green</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y de un carmín encendido:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and of a burning crimson;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Mi verso es un ciervo herido</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my verse is a wounded deer</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Que busca en el monte amparo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that seeks shelter on the mountain.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Cultivo una rosa blanca</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I grow a white rose</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En julio como enero,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in July as in January</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Para el amigo sincero</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for the true friend</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Que me da su mano franca.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who gives me his open hand.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Con los pobres de la tierra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With the poor of the earth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Quiero yo mi suerte echar:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I want to cast my lot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El arroyo de la sierra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the mountain stream</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Me complace más que el mar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>pleases me more than the sea.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: José Martí, Versos sencillos (1891): the verses the song sets, from poems I, V, XXXIX and III (its refrain, credited to Joseíto Fernández, is not printed). Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/Versos_sencillos%2FI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Versos sencillos/I</a>, <a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/Versos_sencillos%2FV" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Versos sencillos/V</a>, <a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/Versos_sencillos%2FXXXIX" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Versos sencillos/XXXIX</a>, <a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/Versos_sencillos%2FIII" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Versos sencillos/III</a>); public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>These are four quatrains of José Martí's Versos sencillos (New York, 1891), from poems I, V, XXXIX and III, the ones most often sung to the tune. Singers choose freely among Martí's quatrains, which is why recordings differ.</li><li>The refrain, &quot;Guantanamera, guajira guantanamera&quot; (country girl from Guantánamo), and the music are 20th-century work credited to Joseíto Fernández and still in copyright: they are not printed here.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
