<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">We Shall Overcome, Pete Seeger</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reworked version on a live album</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Guantanamera, The Sandpipers</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording, international hit</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Guantanamera, Celia Cruz</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1968</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording (Tico Records)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Guantanamera, Wyclef Jean with Celia Cruz, Jeni Fujita and Lauryn Hill</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words and music incorporated</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Caimanera, Robert Wyatt</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording under another title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Albela Rahi, Alamgir</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1980s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adaptation with Urdu words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">British football chants</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, new words</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Versos sencillos, José Martí</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1891</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the verses</span></li></ul></section>
